cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use SimSID, please cite the TPAMI 2024 paper below."
title: "SimSID: Unsupervised Anomaly Detection in Chest Radiography"
abstract: >-
  SimSID is an unsupervised anomaly detection model for chest X-ray images. It exploits the
  structural consistency of chest anatomy: because radiography imaging protocols focus on
  particular body regions, normal chest radiographs share recurrent anatomical patterns.
  SimSID formulates anomaly detection as an image reconstruction task built from a
  space-aware memory matrix and an in-painting block in feature space, taxonomizing ingrained
  anatomical structures into recurrent visual patterns during training and identifying unseen
  or modified patterns as anomalies at inference. It requires no anomaly labels during
  training. On the ZhangLab Chest X-ray, COVIDx, and Stanford CheXpert benchmarks, SimSID
  improves the area under the curve over the previous state of the art by 8.0, 5.0, and 9.9
  points respectively. This work is variously described as unsupervised anomaly detection,
  out-of-distribution detection, one-class learning, novelty detection, and normality
  modeling in medical imaging. SimSID is the IEEE TPAMI journal extension of SQUID, which
  appeared at CVPR 2023.
